; AES_128 ENCRYPTION  the rounds and nothing else  no table and no input or
; output; ENTERED AT CELL 45 with the key in the four word window at the
; block's own zero  the plaintext already in the state  and THE S BOX ALREADY
; IN THE TAPE; It leaves the ciphertext in the state and the pointer at nought;
;
; ENTERED AT 45 AND NOT AT NOUGHT  because 45 is where block/aes128table
; leaves the pointer and cell 45 is the round constant this file's first
; instruction sets; The two were one file until a mode needed the table laid
; down once and the rounds run many times; Splitting them exactly there is
; what keeps this instruction stream identical to the program it came from;
;
; A CALLER MUST HAVE RUN block/aes128table FIRST  and must not run it twice;
; that file's header says why  and the short version is that the lay down is
; delta coded against zeroed cells;
;
; THIS EXISTS SO A MODE CAN HAVE THE CIPHER WITHOUT ITS PROGRAM; aes/encrypt128
; carries an unbalanced walk and so has no INTERFACE line  which means no
; caller can PASTE it; and it is a program besides  reading key and plaintext
; from the wire and emitting sixteen bytes; A mode wants neither of those: it
; wants the rounds over cells it chose itself;
;
; THE KEY IS SPENT; the schedule advances through the window a word at a time
; and the round constant with it  so a caller running a second block must put
; the key back first  and must enter at 45 again with rcon back at zero; That
; is the caller's business and it is why this block does not try to keep a
; pristine copy: a mode knows how many blocks it has and where it can afford
; to keep one; Sixteen bytes against a hundred and fourteen million is not a
; cost worth designing around;
;
; CONTRACTS ARE RELATIVE; every one of them is a claim about a cell of THIS
; block  and the include's offset resolves it; CONVENTIONS section 6 carries
; the rule and tools/bfdag enforces it;
;
; TAPE MAP  (relative to the block's own zero)
;   @0x000:0x00f  window{16}  four schedule slots; the key goes in here
;   @0x010:0x028  the schedule's paste workspace
;   @0x029:0x02c  temp{4}   @0x02d rcon   @0x02e g   @0x02f cnt
;   @0x032:0x061  aes/mixcolumns pasted at 50  state cells 82 to 97
;   @0x052:0x061  state{16}  the plaintext goes in here and the ciphertext
;                 comes back here
;   @0x062:0x071  the round key  copied beside the state each round
;   @0x072:0x374  the S box  laid down by block/aes128table  READ ONLY here
;
; THE ROUND CONSTANT MUST ARRIVE CLEAR; the first instruction below is a
; plus sign on cell 45 and it is meant to make the constant ONE; A caller
; running a second block finds 0x6c there  because the schedule xtimes the
; constant ten times and nothing puts it back; That was measured on the
; Appendix B vector rather than reasoned about  and the contract below is
; what stops the next mode rediscovering it;
;
; ASSERT ptr=+45
; ASSERT zero +45:+45
; the round constant starts at one
  +
  L45
; ASSERT ptr=+0

; ==== round nought : the key alone ====
; the round key is COPIED beside the state; addroundkey spends it and the window is wanted again  for round nought
